# GUI-First Editor Architecture

- Treat the GUI as the primary application shell, not as an optional overlay or separate control window.
- Build toward one editor-style window with first-class regions: Toolbar, Main art viewport, Stats panel, Palette panel, and Status area.
- The Main region owns the rendered LaserBoy art/viewport. Do not draw GUI controls over the art viewport as a substitute for real layout.
- Stats and Status should move out of bitmap-rendered text into real GUI panels/widgets.
- Keep the TUI/keyboard path temporarily for command compatibility and shortcuts; do not preserve it as the long-term primary UI.
- Keep GUI state separate from application state. Mouse state, widgets, panels, selections, and window layout belong in GUI-specific code.
- GUI controls should call existing operations through shared command/action adapters instead of duplicating behavior.
- When an existing keyboard command already performs the desired action, FLTK controls should enqueue that same command event through the GUI command adapter.
- Prefer small, behavior-preserving adapters around existing functions over rewrites or GUI-specific branches inside core algorithms.
